# echo — real-world examples

## Quoting: unquoted vs single vs double

```sh
greeting="hello world"

# Unquoted: variable expands, backslashes need escaping
echo $greeting, blu \(planet\)!
# hello world, blu (planet)!

# Single quotes: everything literal — no expansion
echo '$greeting, blu \(planet\)!'
# $greeting, blu \(planet\)!

# Double quotes: variable expands, most characters literal
echo "$greeting, blu (planet)!"
# hello world, blu (planet)!
```

## Escape sequences with -e

```sh
echo -e "line one\nline two\ttabbed"
```

## No trailing newline

```sh
echo -n "prompt> "
```

## Homelab: quick sanity checks

```sh
# Which shell and host am I on?
echo "$USER@$HOSTNAME running $SHELL"

# Append a line to a config without opening an editor
echo 'alias ll="ls -la"' >> ~/.zshrc

# Exit status of the last command
systemctl is-active plexmediaserver; echo $?
```

| Flag | What it does                     |
| ---- | -------------------------------- |
| -n   | no trailing newline              |
| -e   | interpret \n, \t, etc.           |
| -E   | disable escapes (default)        |
